# Named workload points, so a run's label cannot disagree with its knobs.
#
# The harness itself has no notion of W-base or C11 — a point is identified only by the
# values meta.json records under config.distinctItems / itemsPerOrder / payloadBytes /
# reserveDelayMs. This file is what binds those numbers to a name, once, for every
# variant in the registry.
#
#   POINT=W-hot        scripts/run-suite.sh     # phase 1
#   POINT=W-base,C11   scripts/run-suite.sh     # phase 2 cell
#
# Two classes of value, with different override rules:
#
#   IDENTITY     DISTINCT_ITEMS ITEMS_PER_ORDER PAYLOAD_BYTES RESERVE_DELAY_MS
#                These ARE the point. A conflicting value is FATAL wherever it comes from —
#                the environment, or an earlier point in the same composition. Honouring it
#                is exactly what would make the label lie. `POINT=C01,C11` is therefore
#                rejected (both set PAYLOAD_BYTES, to different values); `POINT=W-base,C11`
#                composes cleanly, because those two share no identity knob. The same value
#                from two points is fine.
#
#   CALIBRATION  STEP_START STEP_INC STEP_COUNT WARMUP_RATE
#                Defaults only; the environment wins silently. The campaign's bracketing
#                rule (design 4.2) expects staircases to be re-tuned and every variant at
#                that point re-run, so locking these would fight the plan. Within a
#                composition the LAST point wins, which is why a cell that re-tunes the
#                staircase must also re-state WARMUP_RATE — see below.
#
# WARMUP_RATE is the rate the fixed-iteration warmup is delivered at, in orders/s. It is a
# point knob rather than a harness default because it has to sit below the SLOWEST
# variant's sustained rate at that point, and those differ by an order of magnitude across
# points. Warming up faster than the system drains is what used to hand T0 a backlog of
# thousands of orders (ES-4-NullLock/W-fan and TO-3-newT/W-hot both blew the 60s settle cap
# that way), so the W values below are set to roughly HALF the slowest sustained rate
# measured by tipping_point.py over bench-results/:
#
#   W-base   slowest sustained 200/s (TO-2)        -> 100/s, 5000 iters = 50s
#   W-hot    not yet bracketed                     ->  50/s, 5000 iters = 100s (conservative)
#   W-fan    slowest sustained  89/s (ES-4)        ->  40/s, 5000 iters = 125s
#
# The C cells carry WARMUP_RATE=0, which is "uncalibrated" and makes k6 refuse the run.
# Every cell that re-tunes STEP_* has a capacity far below the W points it composes with
# (C11's staircase peaks at 29/s), so inheriting W-base's 100/s would silently overload the
# warmup — the exact failure this knob exists to stop. Set a real value on the command line
# when phase 2 is bracketed; a shell-set value wins over the 0 without editing this file.
#
# Sources: campaign design 4.2 (W staircases), 3 (W knobs), 6.1 (cells); the runbook's
# phase-2 staircases (C cells).
#
# <point>  <knobs...>

# W-base   DISTINCT_ITEMS=100 ITEMS_PER_ORDER=4    STEP_START=0 STEP_INC=5 STEP_COUNT=120 STEP_RAMP_S=1 STEP_PLATEAU_S=1 WARMUP_RATE=100
W-base   DISTINCT_ITEMS=100 ITEMS_PER_ORDER=4    STEP_START=0 STEP_INC=5 STEP_COUNT=120 STEP_RAMP_S=15 STEP_PLATEAU_S=15 WARMUP_RATE=100
W-hot    DISTINCT_ITEMS=8   ITEMS_PER_ORDER=4    STEP_START=0 STEP_INC=5 STEP_COUNT=120 STEP_RAMP_S=15 STEP_PLATEAU_S=15 WARMUP_RATE=50
W-fan    DISTINCT_ITEMS=400 ITEMS_PER_ORDER=16   STEP_START=0 STEP_INC=5 STEP_COUNT=120 STEP_RAMP_S=15 STEP_PLATEAU_S=15 WARMUP_RATE=40

C00      PAYLOAD_BYTES=0       RESERVE_DELAY_MS=0
C01      PAYLOAD_BYTES=0       RESERVE_DELAY_MS=25   STEP_START=10 STEP_INC=15 STEP_COUNT=10 WARMUP_RATE=0
C10      PAYLOAD_BYTES=1048576 RESERVE_DELAY_MS=0    STEP_START=5  STEP_INC=5  STEP_COUNT=10 WARMUP_RATE=0
C11      PAYLOAD_BYTES=1048576 RESERVE_DELAY_MS=25   STEP_START=2  STEP_INC=3  STEP_COUNT=10 WARMUP_RATE=0
